  <folder name="Application" id="id-1b7f4bd17a8a4ccca4de4ed0056a7792" type="application">
    <folder name="data objects" id="id-1a54c7e5da3f46c1b129f6b8d854a643">
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Agent" id="id-2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d">
        <documentation>An entoli-agent is an explicitly defined, autonomous software entity within the Entoli ecosystem that, on the basis of a formal agent-charter and agent-contract, executes specific tasks, produces Entoli artifacts or delivers information, and always operates within a demarcated agent-boundary and according to applicable governance.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Agent-intent" id="id-3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e">
        <documentation>An agent-contract formally defines the intent (agent-capability) of the entoli-agent, the input it expects and the output (artifact) it creates or answers it gives, as well as the policies and constraints under which those capabilities are accessible.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Agent-capability" id="id-3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c">
        <documentation>An agent-capability is an explicitly callable function of an entoli-agent, recorded in an agent-contract. Agent-capability is a concrete sub-type of Entoli-capability: it is an Entoli-capability as realised and exposed by one specific agent through its contract.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Artifact" id="id-8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d">
        <documentation>An Entoli artifact is a durable, explicit and transferable recording of result or decision-making, that represents value within a value stream phase and can serve as input for subsequent work.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Artifact-function-axis" id="id-7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c">
        <documentation>The artifact-function-axis is a classification axis for Entoli artifacts that describes which function an artifact fulfils in the decision and value chain within the ecosystem.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Entoli-concept" id="id-039879aaece041a79c21a4d4cbb43d85">
        <documentation>An Entoli concept is the ecosystem-level synonym for Element. Every explicitly defined, coherent and reusable unit of meaning within the Entoli ecosystem is an element in the semantic graph of the canon. The term Entoli-concept is used to refer to any such element by name within ecosystem communication.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Execution" id="id-4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f">
        <documentation>An Execution is a runtime instance derived from an Execution Step Specification. It is the container in which an Instruction Set is carried out and in which Execution Steps are recorded.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Development phase" id="id-0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f">
        <documentation>Development phase is a classification axis that describes in which phase of the formation of work a role or intervention takes place. It positions the work in the chain from intention to operationalisation.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Intervention Type" id="id-1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a">
        <documentation>Intervention Type is the classification axis that defines how an Entoli agent intervenes on artifacts or their meaning.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Prompt" id="id-6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b">
        <documentation>A prompt is a concrete, textual or structured expression of a call or instruction to an entoli-agent, formulated in natural language or another representation form, but itself not forming a canonical part of the agent-contract.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Execution Step" id="id-5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a">
        <documentation>An Execution Step is a runtime record of what occurred at one position within an Execution. It is the instance that came into being when an Execution Step Specification was executed using an Instruction set as LLM input.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Semantic Scope" id="id-a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7">
        <documentation>A Semantic Scope is an explicitly defined, context-specific subset of the Semantic Model, selected for use by a specific agent contract or coordination context.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Semantic Model" id="id-a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6">
        <documentation>The Semantic Model is the complete, authoritative representation of all concepts and their relationships as established in the Entoli Canon.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Source-regime" id="id-9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e">
        <documentation>Source-regime is the classification axis that specifies, per agent-contract, which source space the agent may use during an execution, and thus how strictly derivation is bounded. The source-regime records the concrete rules for that position: which sources are valid, which are excluded, and whether interpretation or synthesis is permitted.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Value stream" id="id-5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e">
        <documentation>A value stream is an explicitly defined chain of value-creating steps that together lead to a recognisable result, and that gives direction to which artifacts, entoli-agents and decisions contribute to that value.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Value stream phase" id="id-4a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d">
        <documentation>A value stream phase is a logically demarcated, value-creating unit within a value stream, in which a specific type of value comes into being, recognisable via explicit artifacts and quality criteria, independent of the manner of execution.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Normative artifact" id="id-e94775ce53da4b1487ade465168978ea">
        <documentation>A normative artifact is a document that prescribes how something must be or be done within the Entoli ecosystem.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Task mode" id="id-ed6c25557b1944309960b3ed32f2463e"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Execution Step Specification" id="id-c8ff51438a5a43e0b2d9f5eb28840a43">
        <documentation>An Execution Step Specification is a predefined, configuration-level description of execution behavior. It specifies which Agent-intent is to be invoked and under what conditions, defined independently of runtime state and independently of LLM input.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Instruction Set" id="id-fd42a7d492c64cbe8d99c4b403501755">
        <documentation>An Instruction Set is an ordered collection of Instructions assembled by coordination and handed off to the execution layer as a single unit of work.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Task Mode" id="id-74040cc1deff420a8806a4db047c18b8">
        <documentation>Task Mode is the data object that records, per agent-intent, which type of operation is to be executed and what output structure must be produced. It carries exactly one enumerated value per agent-intent, drawn from a fixed set of five.&#xD;
&#xD;
It answers a question distinct from the other objects it is associated with: Source Regime records which sources may be loaded; Synthesis Regime records what may be done to meaning; Execution Strategy records how execution progresses; Task Mode records what kind of operation is performed and how the result must be structured.&#xD;
&#xD;
Enumeration values&#xD;
&#xD;
- Extracting: isolate explicitly present elements from source material; output is a list or set of elements; no additional relationships introduced; all elements MUST be directly traceable to source.&#xD;
- Structuring: organise and relate existing elements without altering meaning; output is a structured artifact (e.g. hierarchy, relations, sections); no new meaning or relationships beyond source.&#xD;
- Transforming: reformulate content into a different representation while preserving meaning; output is an equivalent artifact in a new form; semantic equivalence MUST be maintained.&#xD;
- Evaluating: assess content against explicitly defined criteria; output consists of criteria, per-criterion assessment, and explicit conclusion; criteria MUST be stated and traceable.&#xD;
- Originating: produce new content not fully present in the source; output is a coherent artifact fulfilling the task goal; new meaning is allowed only as governed by Synthesis Regime.&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
Constraints&#xD;
&#xD;
- MUST be declared explicitly per agent-intent; never inferred from Source Regime, Synthesis Regime, or Execution Strategy.&#xD;
- MUST define:&#xD;
  - the type of operation performed&#xD;
  - the structure of the output produced&#xD;
- MUST NOT define:&#xD;
  - iteration or looping&#xD;
  - branching or step selection&#xD;
  - progression logic&#xD;
- Extracted from language previously embedded in Interpretation Mode and task descriptions - a relocation and formalisation of existing behaviour, not a new capability.</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Template" id="id-01a25f721c954045b2c20d63486f16f1">
        <documentation>A template is a pre-structured pattern that defines the expected form, sections, or skeleton of a business artifact. It does not prescribe content but provides the structural starting point from which a concrete business artifact can be created. Templates are derived from or aligned with business artifacts and ensure consistency in artifact production across agents and value stream phases.</documentation>
      </element>
    </folder>
    <folder name="application services" id="id-6e3b80b1fc324964a12ef62f53db2397">
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Assemble Instruction Set (with traceability)" id="id-36f6c98ee96744e58d64dd46d233a702"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Define Agent Capabilities" id="id-85df2ac646fe46588e3ae410658daa91"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Execute Instructions (with traceability)" id="id-1f69d183052f4017b64679844b00b0fa"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Provide Normative Rules" id="id-62eb32a5010a493bb7e266d2706af3e2"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Manage User Interaction" id="id-78068053578a4910ac8c2053c6c48370"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Provide Instruction Set" id="id-8dbb220395a243058e0f8b68bb70701f"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Provide Semantic Definitions" id="id-b1c2f8ad3b5c41eb85db9e5ad90b0a57"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationService" name="Provide Service Contracts" id="id-90eb99aba3de40f69d4c769ba6307b6d"/>
    </folder>
    <folder name="components" id="id-483edd418793453b969d483c91d89389">
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Model" id="id-5c60a9024f7d4e049cf444841ac667fd"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Coordination" id="id-e91a272da5ec446a9b29435872d6762f"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Execution" id="id-6bb224a49ad745689e8fff12bc46e137">
        <documentation>Application Service: Deliver Traceable Execution Records&#xD;
&#xD;
Definition:&#xD;
Executes prepared execution contexts and produces traceable artifacts and execution records.&#xD;
&#xD;
Exposed Behavior:&#xD;
&#xD;
Execute service contracts via execution file&#xD;
Invoke LLM and external systems&#xD;
Generate artifacts&#xD;
Register execution traces and provenance&#xD;
&#xD;
Constraint:&#xD;
&#xD;
Must not alter contract semantics&#xD;
Must not reinterpret rules&#xD;
Must ensure full traceability</documentation>
      </element>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Services" id="id-7ca1df8fbfc7435590926b3d73f65ef3"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Studio" id="id-b3e44d6725114de696e460bbb8c39702"/>
      <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Agents (git workspace)" id="id-655f853a6fc641fd9e84d9812794a0f9"/>
    </folder>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Entoli Canon (git workspace)" id="id-ae9c0d0843ce4a6d9fb2faef5951f1e4"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Development phase" id="id-18b7abd4c44941fa91015f313a842f0b">
      <documentation>Development phase is a classification axis that describes in which phase of the formation of work a role or intervention takes place. It positions the work in the chain from intention to operationalisation.&#xD;
&#xD;
POSITIONS DEVELOPMENT PHASE&#xD;
- Exploration — open-ended investigation before commitment&#xD;
- Ordering — structuring and prioritising findings or requirements&#xD;
- Recording — formally capturing decisions, models, or specifications&#xD;
- Testing — verifying and validating against criteria&#xD;
- Accountability — reflecting on and accounting for what has been done&#xD;
- Operationalisation — realising, deploying, or integrating the outcome&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Source-regime" id="id-4906771e6247482ca99b3edec7d325f6">
      <documentation>Source-regime is the classification axis that specifies, per agent-contract, which source space the agent may use during an execution, and thus how strictly derivation is bounded. The source-regime records the concrete rules for that position: which sources are valid, which are excluded,&#xD;
&#xD;
SOURCE REGIME&#xD;
- Input-bound — the agent works from explicitly provided input&#xD;
- Canon-bound — the agent draws from the canonical foundations&#xD;
- Workspace-bound — the agent draws from workspace artefacts&#xD;
- External-source-bound — the agent draws from an external source (web, API)&#xD;
- Open  — Not pre-limited source space; assumptions made explicit (max 3) | Probabilistic |</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Business Artifact" id="id-107ef8b5fd9b446a88363d2b824b1325">
      <documentation>A business artifact is a concrete, durable work product that records content, decisions, analyses, or deliverables produced by an Entoli agent within a value stream phase. It represents substantive output — such as a design, plan, report, code file, or specification — and serves as transferable value within the workspace. A business artifact is one of two subtypes of artifact, distinct from a normative artifact in that it conveys substance rather than prescribing rules or norms.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Representation" id="id-3dc8f4bf9af34905bc794e8e24e37ec1">
      <documentation># Representation within the Workspace&#xD;
&#xD;
A **representation** is the form in which meaning is recorded, displayed, or exchanged.&#xD;
&#xD;
A representation describes *how* something is expressed — not *what* it means and not *what role* it fulfils in the workspace.&#xD;
&#xD;
It is independent of:&#xD;
- the process phase in which something arises (development phase),&#xD;
- the nature of the semantic intervention (intervention type),&#xD;
- the governance role of the artifact (artifact-function-axis).&#xD;
&#xD;
A representation is an **expression form of meaning**.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Definition&#xD;
&#xD;
A representation is a concrete expression of an artifact in a specific form, suitable for human or machine processing.&#xD;
&#xD;
The same meaning can exist in multiple representations without the underlying meaning changing.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Characteristics&#xD;
&#xD;
- Form-bound, not meaning-determining&#xD;
- Can be translated to other representations&#xD;
- Has no normative force of its own&#xD;
- Can be leading or derived (see Derivation Position)&#xD;
- Serves a specific purpose (readability, executability, visualization, processing)&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Typical representation forms&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Text**: Markdown, Plain text, Gherkin, Guideline document&#xD;
- **Diagram**: ArchiMate view, UML diagram, Process diagram, Capability map&#xD;
- **Model data / Code**: JSON, YAML, XML, SQL, Programming language&#xD;
- **Machine representation**: Compiled code, Binary artifacts, Runtime configuration&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Examples&#xD;
&#xD;
| Meaning | Possible representations |&#xD;
|---------|--------------------------|&#xD;
| API contract | OpenAPI (YAML), generated HTML documentation |&#xD;
| Domain model | UML diagram, JSON model, database DDL |&#xD;
| Architecture principle | Markdown document, Wiki page, PDF |&#xD;
| Requirements | Gherkin file, generated test report |&#xD;
&#xD;
In all cases the meaning remains the same while the form differs.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Relation to other ordering concepts&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Artifact-function-axis** determines what the artifact means in the workspace (normative, structuring, realising, descriptive).&#xD;
- **Intervention Type** describes what a role does with meaning.&#xD;
- **Development phase** describes where in the chain it arises.&#xD;
- **Representation** describes exclusively the form in which it exists.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Derivation Position" id="id-b53367de17794d449cc38da23e42092d">
      <documentation>Derivation Position is the classification axis that specifies the positional relationship of a representation to the authoritative source. It records whether a representation is the leading (primary, authoritative) version or a derived (secondary, generated) copy. A leading representation is the source of truth; derived representations are produced from it and must remain consistent with it.&#xD;
&#xD;
POSITIONS DERIVATION POSITION&#xD;
- Leading — the primary, authoritative representation from which others are derived&#xD;
- Derived — a representation produced or generated from the leading representation</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Origin Position" id="id-5c78437e18e544fdb678473ecc70c859">
      <documentation>Origin Position is the classification axis that specifies the initiating context of a business artifact — whether it is created from scratch to start a new thread of work, or built on top of an existing artifact. It records whether an artifact is an initiating artifact (starting a new line of work) or a building-on artifact (extending, refining, or transforming prior work).&#xD;
&#xD;
POSITIONS ORIGIN POSITION&#xD;
- Initiating — the artifact originates a new thread of work without a prior artifact of its type as input&#xD;
- Building-on — the artifact is explicitly derived from or extends an existing artifact</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="LLM engine" id="id-75999b7a0be840a6b98d246ecf2f6e90"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Orchestrator" id="id-4faec7eff3ed4cdf97ac4cd66105a6b5"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Runner" id="id-98c696aa927d4bdd9b01b144881806b9"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationComponent" name="Source Injector" id="id-0a515ab6c3304e2bbf792c6287a94750"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="determine semantic scope" id="id-cb555da8eb3d46ad8ca610f54142172b"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="fetch sources" id="id-4fb28be236234482b601a41bac1353d0"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="build instruction set" id="id-79440920ad744f9fa06c40cac5a01dfc"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="call llm" id="id-c2666a9cff4b464ba5eb5ddafceb462e"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="llm reasoning (interpretation &amp; generation)" id="id-c05ed9b95c994588bb1fa3aac60874a9"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:ApplicationFunction" name="create handoffs" id="id-ca0763694e92497aa88389e424673373"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="normative source" id="id-b5024943d1a14f709b2c0f67df177211"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="working source" id="id-ced93ebf48044ea3b273f54aab51c0d8"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="handoff" id="id-b83f1680d847426d8f503918be294fef"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="large language model" id="id-46cf5218d3164951a8bacc8cc3c5e16f"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="llm-execution-configuration" id="id-d6e9cf7205704d9ba617c4fda8a49057"/>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Synthesis-regime" id="id-bbcbfcd279104c52b55b4973d859dcdd">
      <documentation>Synthesis Regime is the data object that records, per agent-intent, which meaning-transformations the LLM is permitted to perform once its sources are loaded and its task shape is fixed. It carries exactly one enumerated value per agent-intent, drawn from a fixed set of three.&#xD;
&#xD;
It answers a question distinct from the two objects it is associated with: Source Regime records which sources may be loaded; Task Mode records what shape of task this is; Synthesis Regime records what may be done to meaning once both of those are settled.&#xD;
&#xD;
Attributes&#xD;
&#xD;
- value (Enumeration): One of: Faithful, Structuring, Generative&#xD;
- scope (Reference): The agent-intent this value applies to&#xD;
- epistemicGuarantee (Derived boolean): true for Faithful/Structuring, false for Generative unless the generated content has since been validated and canonised&#xD;
&#xD;
Enumeration values&#xD;
&#xD;
- Preserving: no new meaning introduced; rephrasing, summarising, and strict derivation from source only.&#xD;
- Relating: meaning unaltered; only reorganisation, relation-making, or structural exposure of existing content permitted.&#xD;
- Generating: new meaning (hypotheses, assumptions, alternative framings) may be introduced; every such instance must be explicitly marked and carries no epistemic guarantee until validated and canonised.&#xD;
&#xD;
Relationships&#xD;
&#xD;
- Association -> Agent Contract: every agent-intent within a contract carries exactly one Synthesis Regime value.&#xD;
- Association (orthogonal) -> Source Regime: independent axes; an Open source regime does not by itself grant Generative permission.&#xD;
- Association (orthogonal) -> Interpretation Mode: independent axes; the same task shape may be declared under any Synthesis Regime value.&#xD;
- Realization -> Epistemic Guarantee: Faithful and Structuring preserve it on every claim; Generative suspends it for newly introduced content.&#xD;
&#xD;
Constraints&#xD;
&#xD;
- MUST be declared explicitly per agent-intent; never inferred from Source Regime or Interpretation Mode.&#xD;
- Extracted from language previously embedded in Source Regime's Open position and in Interpretation Mode's per-position constraints - a relocation of existing behaviour, not a new permission.&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Execution strategy" id="id-9d7b079582fe4c5e9b58993c759ea4cc">
      <documentation>Execution Strategy is the data object that records, per agent-contract, how Task Modes are selected, sequenced, and iterated during execution. It carries exactly one enumerated value per agent-contract, drawn from a fixed set of three.&#xD;
&#xD;
It answers a question distinct from the other objects it is associated with: Source Regime records which sources may be loaded; Synthesis Regime records what may be done to meaning; Task Mode records what type of operation is performed; Execution Strategy records how execution progresses across those operations.&#xD;
&#xD;
Attributes&#xD;
&#xD;
- value (Enumeration): One of: Linear, Exploratory, Convergent&#xD;
- scope (Reference): The agent-contract this value applies to&#xD;
- progressionDeterminism (Derived enumeration): deterministic for Linear, adaptive for Exploratory, selective for Convergent&#xD;
&#xD;
Enumeration values&#xD;
&#xD;
- Linear: execution follows a predefined sequence of Task Modes; no iteration or branching; progression is fully deterministic and terminates upon completion of the sequence.&#xD;
- Exploratory: execution proceeds iteratively; Task Modes are selected based on intermediate results; progression is adaptive and continues until sufficient understanding or stability is reached.&#xD;
- Convergent: execution begins with multiple candidates (options, hypotheses, alternatives) and reduces them over successive iterations; progression is driven by evaluation and elimination toward a single validated outcome.&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
Constraints&#xD;
&#xD;
- MUST be declared explicitly per agent-contract; never inferred from Task Mode, Source Regime, or Synthesis Regime.&#xD;
- MUST be realised explicitly in orchestration; execution flows must reflect the declared strategy.&#xD;
- MUST NOT define:&#xD;
  - allowed sources&#xD;
  - allowed meaning transformations&#xD;
  - structure of individual tasks&#xD;
- Extracted from previously implicit sequencing, iteration, and decision logic embedded in orchestration flows - a relocation and formalisation of existing behaviour, not a new capability.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Reasoning mode" id="id-53c6d3a2ddf84087bb2f6e66e5ab0334">
      <documentation>Reasning mode is a classification axis that gates whether an agent's work is subject to the content-governance model — the combination of **Source-regime**, **Synthesis-regime**, and **Task Mode** — based on one question: does this step require an LLM to reason at all.&#xD;
&#xD;
Leading question Does this agent-intent require an LLM to reason about meaning, or does it execute by fixed procedure with no reasoning?&#xD;
&#xD;
**Provenance**: replaces the retired **Mode of operation** concept (see Appendix — Deprecated terms). Mode of operation's three positions (Content, Representation-transforming, Conditional) answered three loosely related questions under one label; Determinism asks exactly one, and collapses the two non-content positions — both of which were, on inspection, already mechanical/rule-based rather than meaning-blind-with-exceptions — into a single **Deterministic** position. See `temp/claude-code-plans/plan-execution-nature.md` for the full migration record.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Characteristics ⭐&#xD;
- One of two **agent-intent** classification axes (alongside **Development phase**, which is agent-level — see Development phase, Explanation)&#xD;
- Is a **gate**, not a scale: each position determines *whether* Source-regime/Synthesis-regime/Task Mode apply, not *how much* meaning-transformation is permitted&#xD;
- Independent of other axes, including **Execution Strategy**: Execution Strategy governs how a *sequence* of steps progresses and has no vocabulary for whether one step is mechanical; Determinism governs whether one step requires reasoning at all and has no vocabulary for sequencing. A single Orchestration Specification may freely compose Deterministic and Cognitive steps under any Execution Strategy — neither axis may be inferred from, or used to infer, the other (see Execution Strategy, Relationships)&#xD;
- A **Deterministic** agent-intent is realised as a script, never as an LLM call; a **Cognitive** agent-intent is realised as an LLM call with full content-governance applying&#xD;
&#xD;
### What it is not ❌&#xD;
- Not a meaning-transformation scale (that is **Synthesis-regime**)&#xD;
- Not a task-shape classification (that is **Task Mode**)&#xD;
- Not a progression-logic constraint (that is **Execution Strategy**) — see Characteristics&#xD;
- Not redundant with Synthesis-regime or Task Mode: its positions gate whether those two axes (and Source-regime) apply, they do not duplicate a value on either axis's scale&#xD;
&#xD;
### Synonyms 🏷️&#xD;
- Cognitive gate&#xD;
&#xD;
### Explanation 💬&#xD;
Determinism distinguishes agent-intents that require an LLM to reason from work that is realised as a script and never invokes an LLM at all — mechanical representation-conversion and rule-based condition/hygiene checks alike. Because a Deterministic step never invokes an LLM, it never has content to govern and never has a temperature to calibrate (see `foundations/.canonical/data-model/logical-seed/seed.model.md` §7, collapsed to the Cognitive dimension only as part of this migration).&#xD;
&#xD;
**Binding level**: bound to **agent-intent**, matching Mode of operation's corrected binding level (not Agent) — declared per intent, alongside Source-regime, Synthesis-regime, and Task Mode where those apply.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Positions (Position Elements) 📋&#xD;
&#xD;
| Position | Element classification | Description | Content-governance model applies? |&#xD;
|---|---|---|---|&#xD;
| **Cognitive** | Position Element | Requires an LLM to reason about supplied material | Yes — Source-regime, Synthesis-regime, and Task Mode all apply and MUST be declared |&#xD;
| **Deterministic** | Position Element | Executes by fixed procedure with zero reasoning; realised as a script, never an LLM call | No — not applicable; a script has no Source-regime, Synthesis-regime, or Task Mode to declare, for the same reason it is not an agent-intent in the reasoning sense |&#xD;
</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Artifact (copy)" id="id-e08d942d089147ac92ec1886a6096052">
      <documentation>An Entoli artifact is a durable, explicit and transferable recording of result or decision-making, that represents value within a value stream phase and can serve as input for subsequent work.</documentation>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:DataObject" name="Instruction Set (copy)" id="id-19eff6c74445491e8aaa54b0fc0b69ca">
      <documentation>An Instruction Set is an ordered collection of Instructions assembled by coordination and handed off to the execution layer as a single unit of work.</documentation>
